  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe cooler air has arrived across northeastern Nebraska and \nnorthwestern Iowa. Current temperatures range from the upper 60s to \nmid-70s behind the front, with 80s and 90s ahead of it. A shortwave \nwill enter the region later this afternoon and low pressure is \ncurrently over Iowa. A cold front will continue to push across the \nregion this afternoon. Ongoing showers continue to march across \neastern Nebraska/western Iowa this afternoon, extending from Ceresco \nto Fort Calhoun and Elk Horn (IA). A few thunderstorms are starting \nto pop up near the front in an area with some instability. Should \nany of these storms be able to strengthen, they may produce some \ngusty winds, hail and heavy rain. Additional chances (20-30%) for \nshowers and a few isolated thunderstorms will linger through the \novernight hours. \n\nA disturbance over the Big Horns Sunday morning will progress east \nacross northern Nebraska and the Dakotas through the day, bringing \nadditional chances for some showers and thunderstorms on Sunday and \nMonday. Expected highs will be very fall-like, topping out in the \n50s and 60s. Lows will be a tad chilly, particularly Tuesday \nmorning, dropping into the mid to upper 40s for some and the low 50s \nfor those along and south of I-80.\n\nWeak ridging sets up, moving across the Great Plains and Central \nHigh Plains on Tuesday and Wednesday. Expect a break from \nprecipitation chances and the beginning of a gradual warming trend. \nBy Wednesday afternoon, temperatures will return to the mid-60s for \nmany. Thursday highs return to the mid to upper 60s for many, with a \nfew low 70s to our west. Another disturbance enters the region \nWednesday night/Thursday, bringing chances for a few showers and \nthunderstorms.",
